schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: Smart Contract
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    A program that lives at an address on a blockchain, self-executing its
    coded conditions with no company, government, or individual able to
    alter it once deployed.
  source_traditions:
    - >-
      Term coined by cryptographer Nick Szabo in 1994, decades before the
      technology existed to run one.
    - >-
      First implemented practically with Ethereum's 2015 launch, the first
      blockchain able to run general-purpose smart-contract programs.
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Introduced via the market-stall analogy (two traders, a machine that
    releases payment automatically once delivery conditions are met) to
    ground an abstract concept in a concrete regional trade scenario.
  adjacent_meanings:
    - self-executing agreement
    - on-chain program
  important_distinctions:
    - >-
      Smart contract vs traditional contract: traditional contracts need
      lawyers/courts to enforce terms; a smart contract's code enforces the
      terms automatically.
    - >-
      Smart contract vs oracle: the contract's logic is the closed,
      deterministic part; the oracle is the separate service that feeds it
      real-world data.
  tensions:
    - >-
      Immutability protects against secret rule changes but also means bugs
      are permanent — "code is law" cuts both ways.
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    Framing smart contracts as simply "automatic enforcement" risks eliding
    that most real-world regenerative use cases (verified conservation
    practice, verified shipment) depend entirely on the accuracy of an
    external oracle feed, not the contract logic itself.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/smart-contracts-explained.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
